title: Why the daemon's warnings are routed by repository
tags: [[Connection to the daemon]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: A flush on one repository has no business in the message log of another, nor in any log when no workspace is on that repository.

Each entry of the daemon's diagnostics feed carries a `repo` field. For a long time
no emitter filled it, and the GUI appended every line to every workspace, calling
them all "daemon-wide". With two repositories loaded, the flushes of A filled the
message panel of B, where nothing told them from B's own — which makes the panel
useless as soon as one works on two repositories, and the cause could not be seen
from the GUI: the field existed, it only always looked empty.

So the daemon says which repository a line is about — the watcher and the executor
(a flush's summary, a flush stopped or failed, a watch not placed, the budget spent),
a failed automatic backup — and the GUI routes by it (`crates/gui/src/diagnostics.rs`:
`Line::concerns`).

Three rules follow:

* ''A line about a repository no workspace shows goes nowhere in the GUI'' (user's decision, Nov. 2026). It used to be shown everywhere, on the ground that an error about a repository nobody looks at is the one worth seeing; in practice a loaded repository with no workspace on it — the demonstration one, one borrowed for a sync — filled every message panel with its flushes. The line stays in the daemon's feed (`GET /diagnostics`) and on its stderr (`crates/gui/src/diagnostics.rs`: `recipients`).
* ''A new diagnostic about a repository uses the `_for` variant'' (`diagnostics::warn_for(scope, message, repo)`, and `info_for`, `error_for`) when the repository is at hand.
* ''Deep code uses the plain `warn`/`info`/`error`, inside a repository scope.'' The placement walk, a reconcile's directory listing, a metadata parser, the forest's arbitration know no repository, yet run for one. `diagnostics::in_repo(uuid)` returns a guard under which the plain variants of that thread name the repository; it is entered where the daemon starts working for one — `with_repo` (every request that works on a repository's store), the detached tasks of `routes/fs.rs`, the executor's flush, the watcher's ingest thread, `RepoState::warm`, `load_config` and `refresh_watches`. A diagnostic outside every scope is daemon-wide. The guard is not `Send`, so it cannot be held across an `.await`. Work moved to a thread of its own must enter the scope again: a thread-local does not follow it.

!! Routes on a repository that need no scope

Every route on a repository is about it, but not every one goes through
`with_repo`. That helper is for work on the repository's store: it refuses
a repository still loading (`503`), runs the work on a blocking thread, where it may
wait for the store's lock without holding up the async runtime, times it for the
slow log, and enters the repository scope. Reading a metarecord
(`GET /repos/:repo/records/:uuid`) needs all four; a query needs the scope too, since
a path predicate can make the forest warn that a name denotes two files.

A route that does not touch the store has no use for it, and enters no scope
because it emits no diagnostic (audited Nov. 2026):

* `watch/pause`, `watch/resume` and the watch activity routes flip a flag or read counters in memory. `watch/pause` even must not wait for the store: it has to answer while the flush it stops holds the lock ([[Pausing the watcher]]).
* The repository's info, rename and tasks read memory or write its configuration.
* Init, load, restore, borrow and give-back load the repository through `warm`, which enters the scope itself; unload stops its threads.
* The sync routes, on a pair of repositories, read two stores and the pair's database, and resolve no path. A diagnostic of theirs would have to name its repository explicitly.

A route that comes to reach a reconcile, a watch placement, a path resolution or a
metadata parser off `with_repo` enters `in_repo` itself, as the detached tasks of
`routes/fs.rs` do; one that emits a warning of its own uses `warn_for`.

Filling the field needed no `API_VERSION` bump: it was already in the JSON, always
`null`; giving it a value changes no shape.
